# Brush stacks, a gradient base, and a brushed fill beside a plain stroke, 192x64, three 48px
# squares on paper at y 32.
# Cell 1 (x 32), red with brush:[chip dry]: the edge is bitten by chip's flakes and the inside is
# broken into dry's horizontal streaks, both on the same square.
# Cell 2 (x 96), grad(right gold blue) with dry: the streaks run through a gold-to-blue ramp from
# left to right, so each streak keeps the gradient color under it rather than one flat color.
# Cell 3 (x 160), green fill with stroke:p.ink,3 and brush:dry(on:fill): the fill is dry streaks
# while the ink outline stays a crisp, unbroken 3px square around them.
# The .svg target uses profile:svg: brushed nodes are baked into raster tiles. Cells 1 and 2 match
# the PNG's. Cell 3's tile covers the stroke too, so it starts on a half pixel (x 134.5, y 6.5), and
# the brush samples its noise on a grid shifted by half a pixel: the streaks look the same in style
# and density but fall in different places than in the PNG.
@target brush/stack.png 192x64
@target brush/stack.svg 192x64 profile:svg

use std/brushes { dry chip }

rect 48 at:32,32 fill:p.red.2 brush:[chip dry(strength:.6)] name:stacked
rect 48 at:96,32 fill:grad(right p.gold.2 p.blue.2) brush:dry(strength:.6) name:ramped
rect 48 at:160,32 fill:p.green.2 stroke:p.ink,3 brush:dry(strength:.6 on:fill) name:outlined
